# {{ .Project }}

## Where things live

Specs, plans and SDD live under `docs/.superpowers/`. Flows, each with its
graph picture and an explanation, live under `docs/flows/`.

## Languages

Anything that instructs an LLM is written in English: this file{{ if has "claude" }},
`CLAUDE.md` and whatever lives under `.claude/`{{ end }}{{ if has "antigravity" }},
`GEMINI.md` and whatever lives under `.agents/`{{ end }}. A model reads them on
every turn, and a second language in the file it steers by buys nothing.

So is everything inside a file that is not prose: comments, docstrings and
error messages, in source and in configuration alike — `.loomux/config.toml`{{ if has "claude" }}
and `.claude/settings.json`{{ end }} included. The line runs between prose and
everything else, not between one file type and another; a comment that
explains a regex belongs to the code it sits in, whoever wrote it.

The documentation is bilingual instead: the file without a suffix is written
in `{{ .CommitLanguage }}`, the language of the commit messages, and is the
standard; a translation carries its language code right beside it —
`README.md` / `README.<code>.md`. Each variant links to the other under its
heading.

Exempt from both rules are the working papers under `docs/.superpowers/`:
specs and plans are written once, read by a human, and never translated.

## Configuration

`.loomux/config.toml` is written by a human, never by an agent. It declares
the areas a write barrier trusts and the policy that guards edits. An agent
proposes a change with `loomux config set … --propose` (or `unset`); a human
reviews it with `loomux config proposals` and applies it with
`loomux config apply`.

## Commits

Commit messages are written in `{{ .CommitLanguage }}`.

A commit carries the user as its author and its committer, and nothing in the
message credits a model or an agent — no `Co-Authored-By` line for Claude,
Gemini or a subagent. The history says what changed, not which tool held the
pen.
